The '''Cabinet Office''' ({{Nihongo|内閣府|Naikaku-fu}}) is a cabinet-level administrative organ of the [[Government of Okaiken]] that serves as the primary strategic planning and coordinating body of the [[Executive (government)|executive branch]]. Headed directly by the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] in his capacity as Minister of State for the Cabinet Office, it is responsible for overarching cross-ministerial policy coordination, economic and fiscal policy formulation, national disaster countermeasures, and the administrative oversight of critical statutory commissions including the [[Cabinet Secretariat (Okaiken)|Cabinet Secretariat]], the [[National Public Safety Commission (Okaiken)|National Public Safety Commission]], and the [[Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)|Council for Gender Equality]].

Established on 6 January 2001 as part of the [[2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)|2001 Central Government Reform]], the Cabinet Office was formed through the merger of the [[Prime Minister's Office (Okaiken)|Prime Minister's Office]] (総理府, ''Sōri-fu''), the [[Economic Planning Agency (Okaiken)|Economic Planning Agency]] (経済企画庁, ''Keizai Kikaku-chō''), and regional development secretariats. The structural reform was engineered to reinforce the Prime Minister's capacity for strategic policy initiation and to eliminate systemic ministerial sectionalism (縦割り行政, ''tatewari gyōsei''), wherein individual ministries operated as insular bureaucratic fiefdoms.

With an administrative operational budget of approximately 32 billion Sen and roughly 3,850 specialized personnel, the Cabinet Office operates with a lean permanent establishment compared to line ministries, yet exercises commanding authority due to its statutory role as the Prime Minister's direct policy staff. It houses the [[Chief Cabinet Secretary (Okaiken)|Chief Cabinet Secretary]] and oversees multiple Ministers of State assigned to special missions, including Deputy Prime Minister [[Rento Nakayama]] and Gender Equality Minister [[Shimane Yoshida]]. Under the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]], the Cabinet Office serves as the institutional cockpit executing the administration's core structural commitments: enforcing the Yoshida Threshold, directing the criminal accountability agenda surrounding corporate infrastructure contractors, and coordinating cross-sectoral disaster resilience.

==History and organizational development==

===Pre-reform era (1947–2000)===

Prior to the 2001 reform, overarching coordination across the Sanese executive was fragmented across multiple disparate bodies. The Prime Minister's Office exercised administrative management over honours, decorations, and statistical aggregation, but lacked statutory mechanisms to compel line ministries to adhere to strategic directives. The Economic Planning Agency formulated national five-year growth outlooks and indicative macroeconomic blueprints, yet held no administrative authority over budget allocations or monetary policy.

Throughout the post-war high-growth era, the structural dispersion of coordinating power reinforced bureaucratic supremacy (官僚支配, ''kanryō shihai''). Line departments, particularly Finance, Construction, and International Trade, formulated sectoral legislation with minimal prime ministerial intervention. In 1997, the Administrative Reform Council concluded that modern governance crises—ranging from financial deregulation to catastrophic earthquakes—demanded a centralized prime ministerial organ empowered to dictate basic policy guidelines to the entire bureaucracy.

===2001 Central Government Reform to modern era===

The enactment of the Cabinet Office Establishment Act in 2001 consolidated strategic coordinating machinery within a single supreme office. The legislation endowed the Cabinet Office with explicit authority to formulate national basic policies, establish standing councils chaired by the Prime Minister, and resolve jurisdictional disputes between line ministries.

During the Aoyagi administration (2014–2021), the Cabinet Office was utilized to design social investment strategies and negotiate progressive tax reforms through the Economic and Fiscal Policy Council. Under the subsequent Honda premiership (2021–2026), the office was reoriented toward rapid digital transformation and corporate deregulation, including the incubation of the Digital Agency. However, the twin crises of early 2026—the severe En currency shock and the catastrophic May 2026 Satori AI Blackout—exposed severe coordination breakdowns within the Cabinet Office's crisis response mechanisms, precipitating the fall of the Honda-Nakayama government.

Following the investiture of Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] on 20 May 2026, the Cabinet Office was restructured into a regulatory enforcement and democratic accountability center. The administration immediately instituted the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] via executive order, placed the corporate criminal investigation of [[Kizan Heavy Industries]] under executive monitoring, and codified the binding Yoshida Threshold requiring a minimum 40 percent female cabinet composition.

==Internal organization and policy bureaus==

The Cabinet Office comprises the Minister's Secretariat, policy bureaus, and affiliated policy deliberation councils.

The Minister's Secretariat (大臣官房, ''Daijin Kanbō'') oversees institutional personnel, internal accounts, legislative drafting, and official government press relations. It is directed by the Deputy Director-General (次長, ''Jichō''), the senior career civil servant within the Cabinet Office hierarchy.

The Gender Equality Bureau (男女共同参画局, ''Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Kyoku'') directs national policy on women's empowerment, pay equity, and anti-discrimination. Under Minister [[Shimane Yoshida]], this bureau monitors compliance with the Yoshida Threshold across all public advisory boards, state-owned corporations, and ministerial secretariats.

The Disaster Management Bureau (防災局, ''Bōsai-kyoku'') formulates the Basic Disaster Management Plan and manages the national emergency coordination center during earthquakes, tsunamis, and extreme weather emergencies, linking national defense assets with prefectural rescue services.

The Regional Revitalization Bureau (地方創生局, ''Chihō Sōsei-kyoku'') designs economic countermeasures to combat rural demographic decline and mitigate the hyper-concentration of population and corporate capital within Okami Metropolis.

==Affiliated statutory councils and commissions==

Policy formulation within the Cabinet Office is conducted through high-level consultative councils chaired by the Prime Minister or designated Ministers of State.

